# component: toast
# decisions: A11Y-3, A11Y-4, A11Y-8, A11Y-9, COL-12, COL-23, DOC-1, DOC-2, FOC-1, FOC-6, ICO-8, OVL-13, OVL-15, OVL-17
# hunks:
#   FOC-1, FOC-6: the toast root's `outline-none focus-visible:border-ring
#     focus-visible:ring-[3px] focus-visible:ring-ring/50` halo goes; dropping `outline-none` lets
#     the single 2px `:focus-visible` outline `base.css` owns through.
#   COL-12: `ToastIcon` paints each type in its own status family — `success`, `info` and
#     `warning` gain an ink where upstream left them inheriting the body colour, and `error`
#     moves from the `destructive` FILL to `destructive-text`. The `-text` role is the one that
#     passes AA as ink on a `popover` surface, which is the pair A11Y-13 named in Batch 2.
#   A11Y-9: `tabIndex` follows `aria-hidden`. For a `priority: "high"` toast,
#     `@base-ui/react@1.8.0` (`toast/root/ToastRoot.js`, the `isHighPriority` block) announces a
#     visually hidden `role="alert"` clone in the viewport and marks the VISIBLE root
#     `role="alertdialog" aria-hidden="true"` for as long as the viewport is unfocused, so the
#     polite region does not announce it twice — while leaving `tabIndex: 0` on the root and on
#     its action and close buttons. Nothing tabbable may sit inside an `aria-hidden` subtree, so
#     a keyboard user tabs onto a control no screen reader will name (axe: SERIOUS
#     `aria-hidden-focus`). A11Y-9 is that invariant — what is hidden from assistive technology
#     must not be reachable by keyboard — stated for the modal surfaces; this is the same
#     invariant on a non-modal one, so it is implemented under the existing row rather than a new
#     one. The root's `render` reads `aria-hidden` OFF THE PROPS BASE UI HANDS IT, rather than
#     recomputing `priority === "high" && !focused`, so the hunk keeps matching if upstream
#     changes the condition; it publishes the flag on `ToastHiddenFromAtContext`, and `ToastAction`
#     and `ToastClose` — the focusable parts this file owns — mirror it. `inert` is deliberately
#     NOT used: it would also kill the mouse, and an unfocused error toast's Retry button has to
#     stay clickable. Base UI clears `aria-hidden` the moment the viewport takes focus (F6), so the
#     toast re-enters the tab order exactly when it becomes visible to assistive technology;
#     `toast.test.tsx` walks that round trip and audits the high-priority state with NO axe
#     suppression list, pinning the engine's shape beside it so the hunk fails as stale the day
#     Base UI fixes this. Worth reporting to Base UI.
#   OVL-13: `ToastPortal` reads `useInternalThemeScope()` and wraps its children in a
#     `display: contents` element carrying that class.
#   A11Y-8: NO HUNK. Upstream already signals type with a shape as well as a colour — `ToastIcon`
#     renders a distinct lucide glyph per type, and the COL-12 hunk above only colours glyphs that
#     are already there. `toast.test.tsx` asserts the icon, so a future upstream that drops it
#     fails here.
#   A11Y-3, A11Y-4: NO HUNK. Base UI's `Toast.Viewport` IS the mounted-for-life polite region this
#     policy asks for — `role="region"`, `aria-live="polite"`, rendered by `Toaster` whether or
#     not a toast exists — and per-toast urgency is the manager's `priority: "high"`. Adding a
#     second region would announce every toast twice. `toast.test.tsx` asserts the region's shape.
#   ICO-8: `ToastIcon`'s loading mark is `LoaderIcon` (lucide `loader`) in place of upstream's
#     `Loader2Icon`, which is an alias of `LoaderCircle`. One loader shape across the system —
#     `Spinner`, this icon, Sonner's and `StatusIcon`'s `progress` are the same object spinning.
#     `animate-spin` and `aria-hidden` are upstream's.
#   OVL-17: one toast store. `ToastProvider` marks its subtree with a `ToastProviderScope`
#     context, and `Toaster` given the module `toast` manager renders ONLY its viewport under an
#     existing provider instead of mounting a second one — so under `VegaStackProvider` (which
#     mounts `ToastProvider toastManager={toast}`), `toast()` and `useToastManager()` write to the
#     store the viewport renders, and there is one `[data-slot="toast-viewport"]`. A Toaster given
#     its own manager still brings its own provider.
#   cn: `import { cn } from "cn"` becomes `@vegastack/design` — our npm layer owns `cn` (DOC-2).
#   COL-23: `ToastDescription` gains `first:text-popover-foreground`. Base UI renders
#     `Toast.Title` as `null` when a toast carries no title (`toast/utils/useToastLabelPart.js`,
#     `useToastLabelElement` returns `null` unless the part has renderable children), so upstream's
#     flat `text-muted-foreground` paints a description-only toast's ONLY line — its primary
#     message — in the secondary ink. `first:` is exactly the condition: the description is the
#     first child of the text column precisely when no title rendered before it, and a description
#     that follows a title keeps the muted ink. This is hierarchy, not contrast — `muted-foreground`
#     on `popover` passes AA unaided (contrast-check, 444/444) — and it matches sonner, whose
#     `[data-title]` is `color: inherit` and whose API takes the title as its first argument, so it
#     has no description-only case to get wrong.
#   OVL-15: three extensions to upstream's file, all additive.
#     (a) POSITION. `toastViewportVariants` gives the viewport six logical corners
#         (`top`/`bottom` x `start`/`center`/`end`, default `bottom-end`), replacing upstream's
#         hardcoded `bottom-4 sm:end-4`. Inline names are logical so RTL mirrors without a second
#         vocabulary. Each variant publishes `--toast-dir` (the stack's growth sign), and every
#         vertical term in `toastVariants` is multiplied by it, so ONE set of transforms serves a
#         top- and a bottom-pinned stack; the `var(--toast-dir,-1)` fallback keeps a root composed
#         outside one of our viewports behaving exactly as upstream's did. The discrete flips that
#         are not arithmetic — `top-0`/`bottom-0`, `origin-top`/`origin-bottom` and the
#         pointer-bridging `::after` edge — are the root's `anchor` variant, which `Toaster`
#         derives from `position` along with the swipe direction, so a toast always swipes away
#         from the edge it entered from.
#     (b) ANCHORED TOASTS. `ToastPositioner` and `ToastArrow` wrap the two Base UI parts upstream
#         wraps neither of, so a toast can position against an element via `positionerProps` on
#         `add()` instead of stacking in the corner. The corner-stack recipe therefore moved OUT
#         of `toastVariants`' base and into its `anchor` variant (`TOAST_STACK`), with a third
#         value `none`: a root inside a positioner must not also emit `absolute bottom-0` and
#         the stack transform, or it lands ~27px off the element the positioner placed. `Toast`
#         reads that from `ToastAnchoredContext` rather than asking the caller for a prop, so an
#         anchored composition cannot be built wrong and needs no magic className; an explicit
#         `anchor` still wins. `test/stacking.browser.test.tsx` measures the placement against
#         the anchor after floating-ui settles, and goes red if the context is ignored.
#     (c) CUSTOM BODY. A toast carrying `data.render` renders that instead of the default row,
#         still inside `ToastContent` and a real `Toast` — so it keeps the stack, the swipe,
#         `Escape` and the viewport's live region rather than opting out of them.
#     The `z-60` band the viewport and positioner take is the same row: it is one band above the
#     single `z-50` overlay band, so a toast fired while a Dialog is open is visible instead of
#     behind its scrim. OVL-2 is untouched — nothing else leaves `z-50` — and `z-60` is a plain
#     Tailwind utility, so no `--z-*` token returns.
#   formatting: prettier reflow plus the `registry-stamp.mjs` provenance header (DOC-1); no
#               decision of their own.
